markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Array Methods

#### Think of an array as a row of objects. Need to add one? Remove one? Move something around? That’s what array methods help you do. It is just like updating a playlist or organizing items on a table.

<img src="https://images.code.org/940076172862794f79ba0ed57f648917-image-1765813032852.png" alt="design process graph of the cycle" width="450px" style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;border-radius:10px; "> <div style="background-color: #B7E0E5; border-radius: 10px; padding: 15px; margin: 20px auto; max-width: 85%; box-sizing: border-box; word-wrap: break-word; overflow-wrap: break-word;"> <p style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 12px; line-height: 120%;">📖 An <strong>array method</strong> is a built-in command that lets you modify or use the items in an array. </p> </div>

#### These methods help you add, remove, or rearrange elements in a list.

## Array Methods

<div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px; max-width: 900px; margin: auto;">

  
<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 15px;">
        <img src="https://images.code.org/a05eb6108b405bf7160fd8965c339b7b-image-1765814517494.png" alt="plus sign and arrow" style="width: 20%; max-width: 120px; height: auto; border-radius: 10px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);">
        <div style="flex: 1; background: #f7f8fa; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); font-size: 14pt;">
        <p style = "font-size: 18pt";> <b>push()- adds to the back </b></p>
            <p style = "font-size: 14pt";>            
           (adds an item to the end of the array) <br>
Think: push it to the back of the line
</p>
        </div>
    </div>

   
<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 15px;">
        <img src="https://images.code.org/21a2e31592f9f38eef59fca37004f962-image-1765814621333.png" alt="front arrow and plus sign" style="width: 20%; max-width: 120px; height: auto;border-radius: 10px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);">
        <div style="flex: 1; background: #f7f8fa; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); font-size: 14pt;">
        <p style = "font-size: 18pt";> <b>unshift()- adds to the front </b></p>
            <p style = "font-size: 14pt";>            
           (Adds an item to the front of the array)<br>
Think: shift everything over and put this in front

</p>
        </div>
    </div>

<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 15px;">
        <img src="https://images.code.org/a562ee408b8ddfca5755b9b4de384a14-image-1765814836947.png" alt="minus sign and back arrow" style="width: 20%; max-width: 120px; height: auto;border-radius: 10px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);">
        <div style="flex: 1; background: #f7f8fa; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); font-size: 14pt;">
        <p style = "font-size: 18pt";> <b>pop()- removes from the back </b></p>
            <p style = "font-size: 14pt";>            
           (removes the last item from the array)<br>
Think: pop it off the back.
</p>
        </div>
    </div>

<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 15px;">
        <img src="https://images.code.org/b44a8b88de687b874a9493dcd4e74eb3-image-1765814873469.png" alt="front arrow and minus sign" style="width: 20%; max-width: 120px; height: auto;border-radius: 10px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);">
        <div style="flex: 1; background: #f7f8fa; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); font-size: 14pt;">
        <p style = "font-size: 18pt";> <b>shift()- removes from the front</b></p>
            <p style = "font-size: 14pt";>            
           (removes the first item in the array)<br>
Think: Shift everything up by removing this first item. 

</p>
        </div>
    </div>

---

## Teaching Array Methods 

#### In lesson 3, these array methods are not explicitly taught to the students. Instead, they explore sample code and work with AI tutor to understand these four methods. 

<p style="font-size: 18px; font-weight: bold; background-color: #F5F7F8; padding: 10px; border-left: 5px solid #8148B2; display: inline-block;">
    ➡️ If your class needs a more hands-on way to understand these methods, try the <b>PushPush-Pop Line Game</b> from the <a href='https://example.com/lesson1' target='_blank' rel='noopener noreferrer' style='text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;'>[teacher resources]</a>.
</p>

